🟡 **Medium**

**defaultReadXdgFile catches all errors beyond ENOENT but only warns — a permission error after chmod 600 silently produces 'nothing here'**

The comment says anything other than ENOENT is "worth a diagnostic rather than silently producing the same 'nothing here' result", and a diagnostic is indeed written to stderr. However the function still returns `null` in all error cases, and `resolveCliCredentials` treats a `null` return as "no fallback configured" without any additional context. A user who accidentally did `chmod 000 ~/.config/ai-review/.env` will see `ai-review: failed to read /Users/x/.config/ai-review/.env: EACCES` but then `ai-review watch` will fail with `GITHUB_APP_ID environment variable is required` — a second, disconnected error. The stderr message should say "credentials in this file will not be available" so the user understands the consequence of the read failure without having to connect two messages.

*Suggested fix:*

```suggestion
async function defaultReadXdgFile(): Promise<string | null> {
	try {
		return await readFile(XDG_ENV_PATH, "utf-8");
	} catch (err) {
		// ENOENT (no fallback file configured) is the expected, common case.
		// Anything else — e.g. a permissions error after the docs' recommended
		// `chmod 600` went wrong — is worth a diagnostic rather than silently
		// producing the same "nothing here" result.
		if ((err as NodeJS.ErrnoException).code !== "ENOENT") {
			process.stderr.write(
				`ai-review: failed to read ${XDG_ENV_PATH}: ${errorMessage(err)} — credentials in this file will not be available.\n`,
			);
		}
		return null;
	}
}
```

